FreeBSD's chpass(1), vendored.

Upstream:  freebsd/freebsd-src, releng/15.0
Path:      usr.bin/chpass, plus lib/libc/gen/pw_scan.[ch]
Revision:  e9ac41698b2f322d55ccf9da50a3596edb2c1800 (2024-07-15)

WHY IT IS HERE

NextBSD's chpass edits the two fields a directory account has. An account in
/etc/master.passwd has the rest -- login class, password ageing, expiry -- and
editing those means the $EDITOR session this front end deliberately does not
implement. Rather than reimplement that editor over fields the property lists
do not model, the account gets the chpass it has always had.

It used to be exec'd as a second binary that compat relocated to
/usr/libexec/bsd/chpass. See ../../pw/bsd/README for why that arrangement went.

WHAT WAS CHANGED

One file, and one line in it:

  chpass.c -> bsd_main.c, and its main() renamed to chpass_bsd_main().

pw_scan.c and pw_scan.h are copied from lib/libc/gen because upstream's build
reaches into the libc source tree for them; they are unmodified.

NIS is left out. -DYP is never defined, so the ypclnt paths compile out, and
the front end refuses the yp* names before reaching this code at all.

Not built on Darwin: libutil's pw_* and login_cap do not exist there. The
front end stubs chpass_bsd_main() out so a host syntax check still compiles.

RE-SYNCING

  1. Fetch the new upstream usr.bin/chpass and lib/libc/gen/pw_scan.[ch].
  2. Diff against this directory, remembering bsd_main.c is upstream's chpass.c.
  3. Apply, re-apply the main() rename, and update Revision: above.
